{"type": "tool_call", "id": "tool-receipt:07e264f275e6df35297d1790", "attempt_id": "attempt:22da415b81a4f067ba9b9285", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator"}, "result": "## Symplectic integrator - Wikipedia\n\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator\n\n---\n\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#bodyContent)\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nNumerical integration scheme for Hamiltonian systems\n\nIn[mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematics), a**symplectic integrator**(**SI**) is a[numerical integration scheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_ordinary_differential_equations)for[Hamiltonian systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_system). Symplectic integrators form the subclass of[geometric integrators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geometric_integrator)which, by definition, are[canonical transformations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonical_transformation). They are widely used in[nonlinear dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonlinear_dynamics),[molecular dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_dynamics),[discrete element methods](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_element_method),[accelerator physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle_accelerator),[plasma physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plasma_physics),[quantum physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_physics), and[celestial mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celestial_mechanics).\n\n## Introduction[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=1)]\n\nSymplectic integrators are designed for the numerical solution of[Hamilton's equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton's_equations), which readp˙=−∂H∂qandq˙=∂H∂p,{\\displaystyle {\\dot {p}}=-{\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial q}}\\quad {\\mbox{and}}\\quad {\\dot {q}}={\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial p}},}whereq{\\displaystyle q}denotes the position coordinates,p{\\displaystyle p}the momentum coordinates, andH{\\displaystyle H}is the Hamiltonian. The set of position and momentum coordinates(q,p){\\displaystyle (q,p)}are called[canonical coordinates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonical_coordinates). (See[Hamiltonian mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_mechanics)for more background.)\n\nThe[time evolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_evolution)of[Hamilton's equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton's_equations)is a[symplectomorphism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectomorphism), meaning that it conserves the symplectic[2-form](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2-form)dp∧dq{\\displaystyle dp\\wedge dq}. A numerical scheme is a symplectic integrator if it also conserves this 2-form.\n\nSymplectic integrators possess, as a conserved quantity, a Hamiltonian which is slightly[perturbed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perturbation_theory)from the original one.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-1)By virtue of these advantages, the SI scheme has been widely applied to the calculations of long-term evolution of chaotic Hamiltonian systems ranging from the[Kepler problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kepler_problem)to the classical and semi-classical simulations in[molecular dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_dynamics).\n\nMost of the usual numerical methods, such as the primitive[Euler scheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_integration)and the classical[Runge–Kutta scheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runge%E2%80%93Kutta_methods), are not symplectic integrators.\n\n## Methods for constructing symplectic algorithms[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=2)]\n\n### Splitting methods for separable Hamiltonians[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=3)]\n\nA widely used class of symplectic integrators is formed by the splitting methods.\n\nAssume that the Hamiltonian is separable, meaning that it can be written in the formH(p,q)=T(p)+V(q).{\\displaystyle H(p,q)=T(p)+V(q).}1\n\nThis happens frequently in Hamiltonian mechanics, withTbeing the[kinetic energy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinetic_energy)andVthe[potential energy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potential_energy).\n\nFor the notational simplicity, let us introduce the symbolz=(q,p){\\displaystyle z=(q,p)}to denote the canonical coordinates including both the position and momentum coordinates. Then, the set of the Hamilton's equations given in the introduction can be expressed in a single expression asz˙={z,H(z)},{\\displaystyle {\\dot {z}}=\\{z,H(z)\\},}2\n\nwhere{⋅,⋅}{\\displaystyle \\{\\cdot ,\\cdot \\}}is a[Poisson bracket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson_bracket). Furthermore, by introducing an operatorDH⋅={⋅,H}{\\displaystyle D_{H}\\cdot =\\{\\cdot ,H\\}}, which returns a[Poisson bracket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson_bracket)of the operand with the[Hamiltonian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_mechanics#Mathematical_formalism), the expression of the Hamilton's equation can be further simplified to\n\nz˙=DHz.{\\displaystyle {\\dot {z}}=D_{H}z.}\n\nThe formal solution of this set of equations at timet{\\displaystyle t}is given as a[matrix exponential](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matrix_exponential):z(t)=exp⁡(tDH)z(0).{\\displaystyle z(t)=\\exp(tD_{H})\\,z(0).}3\n\nNote the positivity oftDH{\\displaystyle tD_{H}}in the matrix exponential.\n\nWhen the Hamiltonian has the form of equation (**[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_1)**), the solution (**[3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_3)**) is equivalent toz(t)=exp⁡[t(DT+DV)]z(0).{\\displaystyle z(t)=\\exp \\left[t(D_{T}+D_{V})\\right]\\,z(0).}4\n\nThe SI scheme approximates the time-evolution operatorexp⁡[t(DT+DV)]{\\displaystyle \\exp[t(D_{T}+D_{V})]}in the formal solution (**[4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_4)**) by a product of operators asexp⁡[t(DT+DV)]=∏i=k,…,1exp⁡(tdiDT)exp⁡(tciDV)+O(tk+1)=exp⁡(tdkDT)exp⁡(tckDV)⋯exp⁡(td1DT)exp⁡(tc1DV)+O(tk+1),{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\exp[t(D_{T}+D_{V})]&=\\prod _{i=k,\\dots ,1}\\exp(td_{i}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{i}D_{V})+O(t^{k+1})\\\\&=\\exp(td_{k}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{k}D_{V})\\cdots \\exp(td_{1}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{1}D_{V})+O(t^{k+1}),\\end{aligned}}}5\n\nwhereci{\\displaystyle c_{i}}anddi{\\displaystyle d_{i}}are real numbers,k{\\displaystyle k}is an integer, which is called the order of the integrator, and where∑i=1kci=∑i=1kdi=1{\\textstyle \\sum _{i=1}^{k}c_{i}=\\sum _{i=1}^{k}d_{i}=1}. Note that each of the operatorsexp⁡(tciDV){\\displaystyle \\exp(tc_{i}D_{V})}andexp⁡(tdiDT){\\displaystyle \\exp(td_{i}D_{T})}provides a[symplectic map](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectomorphism), so their product appearing in the right-hand side of (**[5](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_5)**) also constitutes a symplectic map.\n\nSinceDT2z={{z,T},T}={(q˙,0),T}=(0,0){\\displaystyle D_{T}^{2}z=\\{\\{z,T\\},T\\}=\\{({\\dot {q}},0),T\\}=(0,0)}for allz{\\displaystyle z}, we can conclude thatDT2=0.{\\displaystyle D_{T}^{2}=0.}6\n\nBy using a[Taylor series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taylor_series),exp⁡(aDT){\\displaystyle \\exp(aD_{T})}can be expressed asexp⁡(aDT)=∑n=0∞(aDT)nn!,{\\displaystyle \\exp(aD_{T})=\\sum _{n=0}^{\\infty }{\\frac {{\\left(aD_{T}\\right)}^{n}}{n!}},}7\n\nwherea{\\displaystyle a}is an arbitrary[real number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_number). Combining (**[6](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_6)**) and (**[7](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_7)**), and by using the same reasoning forDV{\\displaystyle D_{V}}as we have used forDT{\\displaystyle D_{T}}, we get{exp⁡(aDV)=1+aDV,exp⁡(aDT)=1+aDT.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{cases}\\exp(aD_{V})=1+aD_{V},\\\\[2pt]\\exp(aD_{T})=1+aD_{T}.\\end{cases}}}8\n\nIn concrete terms,exp⁡(tciDV){\\displaystyle \\exp(tc_{i}D_{V})}gives the mapping\n\n(qp)↦(qp−tci∂V∂q(q)),{\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}q\\\\p\\end{pmatrix}}\\mapsto {\\begin{pmatrix}q\\\\p-tc_{i}{\\frac {\\partial V}{\\partial q}}(q)\\end{pmatrix}},}\n\nandexp⁡(tdiDT){\\displaystyle \\exp(td_{i}D_{T})}gives\n\n(qp)↦(q+tdi∂T∂p(p)p).{\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}q\\\\p\\end{pmatrix}}\\mapsto {\\begin{pmatrix}q+td_{i}{\\frac {\\partial T}{\\partial p}}(p)\\\\p\\end{pmatrix}}.}\n\nNote that both of these maps are practically computable.\n\n#### Algorithm[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=4)]\n\nIn each time step, the general algorithm for this class of symplectic integrators maps some initial state(q0,p0){\\displaystyle (q_{0},p_{0})}to the state(qk,pk){\\displaystyle (q_{k},p_{k})}one timestept{\\displaystyle t}later. This is done through the sequence of substeps(q0,p0)↦(q1,p1)↦⋯↦(qk−1,pk−1)↦(qk,pk){\\displaystyle (q_{0},p_{0})\\mapsto (q_{1},p_{1})\\mapsto \\dots \\mapsto (q_{k-1},p_{k-1})\\mapsto (q_{k},p_{k})}. For everyi=1,…,k{\\displaystyle i=1,\\dots ,k}, the following equations are used to compute the substep(qi−1,pi−1)↦(qi,pi){\\displaystyle (q_{i-1},p_{i-1})\\mapsto (q_{i},p_{i})}:\n\npi=pi−1+tciF(qi−1)qi=qi−1+tdipim{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}p_{i}&=p_{i-1}+tc_{i}F(q_{i-1})\\\\[1ex]q_{i}&=q_{i-1}+td_{i}{\\frac {p_{i}}{m}}\\end{aligned}}}\n\nwhereq{\\displaystyle q}is the position,p{\\displaystyle p}is the momentum,F(q)=−∂V∂q(q){\\displaystyle F(q)=-{\\tfrac {\\partial V}{\\partial q}}(q)}is the force vector atq{\\displaystyle q}, andm{\\displaystyle m}is the scalar quantity of mass.\n\nEquivalently, after converting into Lagrangian coordinates, each substep becomes\n\nvi=vi−1+tcia(xi−1)xi=xi−1+tdivi{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}v_{i}&=v_{i-1}+tc_{i}a(x_{i-1})\\\\[1ex]x_{i}&=x_{i-1}+td_{i}v_{i}\\end{aligned}}}\n\nwherex{\\displaystyle x}is the position,v{\\displaystyle v}is the velocity, anda(x){\\displaystyle a(x)}is the acceleration vector atx{\\displaystyle x}.\n\nSeveral symplectic integrators are given below.\n\n#### A first-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=5)]\n\nThe[symplectic Euler method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_Euler_method)is the first-order integrator withk=1{\\displaystyle k=1}and coefficientsc1=d1=1.{\\displaystyle c_{1}=d_{1}=1.}\n\nNote that the algorithm above does not work if time-reversibility is needed. The algorithm has to be implemented in two parts, one for positive time steps, one for negative time steps.\n\n#### A second-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=6)]\n\nThe[Verlet method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verlet_integration)is the second-order symplectic integrator withk=2{\\displaystyle k=2}and coefficients\n\nc1=12,d1=1,c2=12,d2=0.{\\displaystyle c_{1}={\\tfrac {1}{2}},\\qquad d_{1}=1,\\qquad c_{2}={\\tfrac {1}{2}},\\qquad d_{2}=0.}\n\nNote that the coefficients\n\nc1=0,d1=12,c2=1,d2=12{\\displaystyle c_{1}=0,\\qquad d_{1}={\\tfrac {1}{2}},\\qquad c_{2}=1,\\qquad d_{2}={\\tfrac {1}{2}}}\n\nalso form a second-order symplectic integrator[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-forest1990-2)[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-yoshida1990-3), which is the Verlet method but shifted by half a time stept{\\displaystyle t}.\n\nSinced2=0{\\displaystyle d_{2}=0}in the first set of coefficients andc1=0{\\displaystyle c_{1}=0}in the second set of coefficients, in both algorithms above one update can be ignored. There are thus only 3 steps to the algorithm, and step 1 and 3 are exactly the same, leading to the algorithm being symmetric in time, so the positive time version can also be used for negative time.\n\n#### A third-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=7)]\n\nA third-order symplectic integrator (withk=3{\\displaystyle k=3}) was discovered by Ronald Ruth in 1983.[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-4)One of the many solutions is given byc1=724,c2=34,c3=−124,d1=23,d2=−23,d3=1.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}c_{1}&={\\tfrac {7}{24}},&c_{2}&={\\tfrac {3}{4}},&c_{3}&=-{\\tfrac {1}{24}},\\\\[1ex]d_{1}&={\\tfrac {2}{3}},&d_{2}&=-{\\tfrac {2}{3}},&d_{3}&=1.\\end{aligned}}}\n\n#### A fourth-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=8)]\n\nA fourth-order integrator (withk=4{\\displaystyle k=4}) was also discovered by Ruth in 1983 and distributed privately to the particle-accelerator community at that time. This was described in a lively review article by Forest.[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-5)This fourth-order integrator was published in 1990 by Forest and Ruth and also independently discovered by two other groups around that same time:[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-forest1990-2)[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-yoshida1990-3)[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-6)c1=c4=12(2−21/3),c2=c3=1−21/32(2−21/3),d1=d3=12−21/3,d2=−21/32−21/3,d4=0,{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}c_{1}&=c_{4}={\\frac {1}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}},&c_{2}&=c_{3}={\\frac {1-2^{1/3}}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}},\\\\[1ex]d_{1}&=d_{3}={\\frac {1}{2-2^{1/3}}},&d_{2}&=-{\\frac {2^{1/3}}{2-2^{1/3}}},\\qquad d_{4}=0,\\end{aligned}}}or alternatively,c1=0,c3=−21/32−21/3,c2=c4=12−21/3,d1=d4=12(2−21/3),d2=d3=1−21/32(2−21/3).{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}c_{1}&=0,\\qquad c_{3}=-{\\frac {2^{1/3}}{2-2^{1/3}}},&c_{2}&=c_{4}={\\frac {1}{2-2^{1/3}}},\\\\[1ex]d_{1}&=d_{4}={\\frac {1}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}},&d_{2}&=d_{3}={\\frac {1-2^{1/3}}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}}.\\end{aligned}}}\n\nTo determine these coefficients, the[Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baker%E2%80%93Campbell%E2%80%93Hausdorff_formula)can be used.[Yoshida](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haruo_Yoshida?action=edit&redlink=1), in particular, gives an elegant derivation of coefficients for[higher-order integrators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leapfrog_integration#Yoshida_algorithms).[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-yoshida1990-3)Later on, Blanes and Moan[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-7)further developed partitioned[Runge–Kutta methods](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runge%E2%80%93Kutta_methods)for the integration of systems with separable Hamiltonians with very small error constants.[Numerov's method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerov's_method), developed by the Russian astronomer[Boris Vasil'evich Numerov](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boris_Vasil'evich_Numerov)in 1924, is also a fourth-order symplectic integrator.[[8]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-8)\n\n### Splitting methods for general nonseparable Hamiltonians[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=9)]\n\nGeneral nonseparable Hamiltonians can also be explicitly and symplectically integrated.\n\nTo do so, Tao introduced a restraint that binds two copies of phase space together to enable an explicit splitting of such systems.[[9]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-9)The idea is, instead ofH(Q,P){\\displaystyle H(Q,P)}, one simulatesH¯(q,p,x,y)=H(q,y)+H(x,p)+ω(12‖q−x‖22+12‖p−y‖22),{\\displaystyle {\\bar {H}}(q,p,x,y)=H(q,y)+H(x,p)+\\omega \\left({\\tfrac {1}{2}}{\\left\\|q-x\\right\\|}_{2}^{2}+{\\tfrac {1}{2}}{\\left\\|p-y\\right\\|}_{2}^{2}\\right),}whose solution agrees with that ofH(Q,P){\\displaystyle H(Q,P)}in the sense thatq(t)=x(t)=Q(t){\\displaystyle q(t)=x(t)=Q(t)},p(t)=y(t)=P(t){\\displaystyle p(t)=y(t)=P(t)}.\n\nThe new Hamiltonian is advantageous for explicit symplectic integration, because it can be split into the sum of three sub-Hamiltonians,HA=H(q,y){\\displaystyle H_{A}=H(q,y)},HB=H(x,p){\\displaystyle H_{B}=H(x,p)}, andHC=ω(12‖q−x‖22+12‖p−y‖22){\\textstyle H_{C}=\\omega \\left({\\frac {1}{2}}\\left\\|q-x\\right\\|_{2}^{2}+{\\frac {1}{2}}\\left\\|p-y\\right\\|_{2}^{2}\\right)}. Exact solutions of all three sub-Hamiltonians can be explicitly obtained: bothHA,HB{\\displaystyle H_{A},H_{B}}solutions correspond to shifts of mismatched position and momentum, andHC{\\displaystyle H_{C}}corresponds to a linear transformation. To symplectically simulate the system, one simply composes these solution maps.\n\n## Applications[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=10)]\n\n### In plasma physics[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=11)]\n\nIn recent decades symplectic integrator in plasma physics has become an active research topic,[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-10)because straightforward applications of the standard symplectic methods do not suit the need of large-scale plasma simulations enabled by the peta- to exa-scale computing hardware. Special symplectic algorithms need to be customarily designed, tapping into the special structures of the physics problem under investigation. One such example is the[charged particle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charged_particle)dynamics in an[electromagnetic field](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_field). With the canonical symplectic structure, the Hamiltonian of the dynamics isH(p,x)=12(p−A)2+ϕ,{\\displaystyle H({\\boldsymbol {p}},{\\boldsymbol {x}})={\\tfrac {1}{2}}\\left({\\boldsymbol {p}}-{\\boldsymbol {A}}\\right)^{2}+\\phi ,}whosep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence andx{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {x}}}-dependence are not separable, and standard explicit symplectic methods do not apply. For large-scale simulations on massively parallel clusters, however, explicit methods are preferred. To overcome this difficulty, we can explore the specific way that thep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence andx{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {x}}}-dependence are entangled in this Hamiltonian, and try to design a symplectic algorithm just for this or this type of problem. First, we note that thep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence is quadratic, therefore the first order symplectic Euler method implicit inp{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}is actually explicit. This is what is used in the canonical symplectic[particle-in-cell](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle-in-cell)(PIC) algorithm.[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-11)To build high order explicit methods, we further note that thep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence andx{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {x}}}-dependence in thisH(p,x){\\textstyle H({\\boldsymbol {p}},{\\boldsymbol {x}})}are product-separable, 2nd and 3rd order explicit symplectic algorithms can be constructed using generating functions,[[12]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-12)and arbitrarily high-order explicit symplectic integrators for time-dependent electromagnetic fields can also be constructed using Runge-Kutta techniques.[[13]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-13)\n\nA more elegant and versatile alternative is to look at the following non-canonical symplectic structure of the problem,i(x˙,v˙)Ω=−dH,Ω=d(v+A)∧dx,H=12v2+ϕ.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}i_{({\\dot {\\boldsymbol {x}}},{\\dot {\\boldsymbol {v}}})}\\Omega &=-dH,\\\\\\Omega &=d({\\boldsymbol {v}}+{\\boldsymbol {A}})\\wedge d{\\boldsymbol {x}},\\\\[1ex]H&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}{\\boldsymbol {v}}^{2}+\\phi .\\end{aligned}}}HereΩ{\\textstyle \\Omega }is a non-constant non-canonical symplectic form. General symplectic integrator for non-constant non-canonical symplectic structure, explicit or implicit, is not known to exist. However, for this specific problem, a family of high-order explicit non-canonical symplectic integrators can be constructed using the He splitting method.[[14]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-14)SplittingH{\\textstyle H}into 4 parts,H=Hx+Hy+Hz+Hϕ,{\\displaystyle H=H_{x}+H_{y}+H_{z}+H_{\\phi },}Hx=12vx2,Hy=12vy2,Hz=12vz2,Hϕ=ϕ,{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}H_{x}&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}v_{x}^{2},&H_{y}&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}v_{y}^{2},\\\\[2pt]H_{z}&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}v_{z}^{2},&H_{\\phi }&=\\phi ,\\end{aligned}}}we find serendipitously that for each subsystem, e.g.,i(x˙,v˙)Ω=−dHx{\\displaystyle i_{({\\dot {\\boldsymbol {x}}},{\\dot {\\boldsymbol {v}}})}\\Omega =-dH_{x}}andi(x˙,v˙)Ω=−dHϕ,{\\displaystyle i_{({\\dot {\\boldsymbol {x}}},{\\dot {\\boldsymbol {v}}})}\\Omega =-dH_{\\phi },}the solution map can be written down explicitly and calculated exactly. Then explicit high-order non-canonical symplectic algorithms can be constructed using different compositions. LetΘx,Θy,Θz{\\textstyle \\Theta _{x},\\Theta _{y},\\Theta _{z}}andΘϕ{\\textstyle \\Theta _{\\phi }}denote the exact solution maps for the 4 subsystems. A 1st-order symplectic scheme isΘ1(Δτ)=Θx(Δτ)Θy(Δτ)Θz(Δτ)Θϕ(Δτ).{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\Theta _{1}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}=\\Theta _{x}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,\\Theta _{y}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,\\Theta _{z}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,\\Theta _{\\phi }{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,.\\end{aligned}}}A symmetric 2nd-order symplectic scheme is,Θ2(Δτ)=Θx(Δτ2)Θy(Δτ2)Θz(Δτ2)Θϕ(Δτ)Θz(Δt2)Θy(Δt2)Θx(Δt2),{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\Theta _{2}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}={}&\\Theta _{x}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta \\tau }{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{y}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta \\tau }{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{z}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta \\tau }{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{\\phi }{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\\\&\\Theta _{z}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta t}{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{y}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta t}{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{x}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta t}{2}}\\right)},\\end{aligned}}}which is a customarily modified[Strang splitting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strang_splitting). A2(ℓ+1){\\textstyle 2(\\ell +1)}-th order scheme can be constructed from a2ℓ{\\textstyle 2\\ell }-th order scheme using the method of triple jump,Θ2(ℓ+1)(Δτ)=Θ2ℓ(αℓΔτ)Θ2ℓ(βℓΔτ)Θ2ℓ(αℓΔτ),{\\displaystyle \\Theta _{2(\\ell +1)}(\\Delta \\tau )=\\Theta _{2\\ell }(\\alpha _{\\ell }\\Delta \\tau )\\,\\Theta _{2\\ell }(\\beta _{\\ell }\\Delta \\tau )\\,\\Theta _{2\\ell }(\\alpha _{\\ell }\\Delta \\tau )\\,,}αℓ=12−21/(2ℓ+1),βℓ=1−2αℓ.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\alpha _{\\ell }&={\\frac {1}{2-2^{1/(2\\ell +1)}}},&\\beta _{\\ell }&=1-2\\alpha _{\\ell }\\,.\\end{aligned}}}The He splitting method is one of key techniques used in the structure-preserving geometric[particle-in-cell](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle-in-cell)(PIC) algorithms.[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-15)[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-16)[[17]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-17)[[18]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-18)\n\n## See also[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=12)]\n- [Energy drift](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_drift)\n- [Multisymplectic integrator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multisymplectic_integrator)\n- [Variational integrator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variational_integrator)\n- [Verlet integration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verlet_integration)\n\n## References[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=13)]\n\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_ref-1)Tuckerman, Mark E. (2010).*Statistical Mechanics: Theory and Molecular Simulation*(1ed.). Oxford Universit