{"type": "attempt_input", "content": {"input": {"question": "Gravity keeps planets in stable orbits around the sun. Yet orbits do decay very gradually. Eventually, the planets will swirl into the sun.", "direction": {"title": "Baseline Conservative Dynamics Verification", "rationale": "Isolate pure Newtonian gravity in a Sun-Earth two-body system to establish a zero-dissipation baseline and quantify numerical versus physical orbital drift.", "workflow": "simulation", "completion_test": "High-precision integrator confirms semi-major axis conservation within floating-point tolerance over 10^6 years, confirming gravity alone does not cause decay.", "remaining": "Selection of the first non-conservative perturbation (tidal, drag, or radiation) to introduce into the model based on dominant timescale analysis.", "position": 0}}}}
{"type": "attempt_output", "content": {"output": {"queries": ["symplectic integration numerical conservation semi-major axis long-term celestial mechanics", "numerical versus physical orbital drift conservative Newtonian two-body floating-point tolerance"], "sources": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:229c73e93bc57af4dc9897d9d76116b332d31c8c5210882bb2af043dbb3dabb3", "artifact_id": "artifact:7e2cb13a51300c867d152c217fa2cf038a8fdc76ce3254e9334ddb88d0a6c780", "sha256": "7e2cb13a51300c867d152c217fa2cf038a8fdc76ce3254e9334ddb88d0a6c780", "title": "Symplectic integrators for long-term integrations in celestial ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF00048485.pdf", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 8}, "content": "## Client Challenge\n\n**Source**: https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF00048485.pdf\n\n---\n\nA required part of this site couldn’t load. This may be due to a browser extension, network issues, or browser settings. Please check your connection, disable\nany ad blockers, or try using a different browser."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:286f44df09a9cd47cb8c6522c396d376a750d051d4446dba9218c2a636fe4f9d", "artifact_id": "artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "title": "(PDF) Symplectic integrators for long-term integrations in celestial ...", "url": "https://www.academia.edu/22201129/Symplectic_integrators_for_long_term_integrations_in_celestial_mechanics", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:6c5e5d5aa3089fb50f07b3da6648e7f11186efb0556ac9ed8010748287991f7a", "artifact_id": "artifact:9e3657f36b843ebbdca033e6fe46ad99d477949cab629e26b9ff88d5d8757ba8", "sha256": "9e3657f36b843ebbdca033e6fe46ad99d477949cab629e26b9ff88d5d8757ba8", "title": "Symplectic integrators for long-term integrations in celestial ...", "url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1991CeMDA..52..221G/abstract", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_invalid_content\nResponse content is not valid for extraction."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:98a884f4e9b3697e7ee5ae56fc650c9620ecae4131c08275493805420cd7257f", "artifact_id": "artifact:adc6caa739d4d259862d0be45fdead67c68cf5a58a843413f771125b8011263e", "sha256": "adc6caa739d4d259862d0be45fdead67c68cf5a58a843413f771125b8011263e", "title": "Symplectic integrator - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 401}, "content": "## Symplectic integrator - Wikipedia\n\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator\n\n---\n\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#bodyContent)\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nNumerical integration scheme for Hamiltonian systems\n\nIn[mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematics), a**symplectic integrator**(**SI**) is a[numerical integration\nscheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_ordinary_differential_equations)for[Hamiltonian systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_system).\nSymplectic integrators form the subclass of[geometric integrators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geometric_integrator)which, by definition, are[canonical\ntransformations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonical_transformation). They are widely used in[nonlinear\ndynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonlinear_dynamics),[molecular dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_dynamics),[discrete element\nmethods](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_element_method),[accelerator physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle_accelerator),[plasma\nphysics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plasma_physics),[quantum physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_physics), and[celestial\nmechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celestial_mechanics).\n\n## Introduction[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=1)]\n\nSymplectic integrators are designed for the numerical solution of[Hamilton's equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton's_equations), which\nreadp˙=−∂H∂qandq˙=∂H∂p,{\\displaystyle {\\dot {p}}=-{\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial q}}\\quad {\\mbox{and}}\\quad {\\dot {q}}={\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial\np}},}whereq{\\displaystyle q}denotes the position coordinates,p{\\displaystyle p}the momentum coordinates, andH{\\displaystyle H}is the Hamiltonian. The set of\nposition and momentum coordinates(q,p){\\displaystyle (q,p)}are called[canonical coordinates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonical_coordinates).\n(See[Hamiltonian mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_mechanics)for more background.)\n\nThe[time evolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_evolution)of[Hamilton's equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton's_equations)is\na[symplectomorphism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectomorphism), meaning that it conserves the\nsymplectic[2-form](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2-form)dp∧dq{\\displaystyle dp\\wedge dq}. A numerical scheme is a symplectic integrator if it also conserves\nthis 2-form.\n\nSymplectic integrators possess, as a conserved quantity, a Hamiltonian which is slightly[perturbed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perturbation_theory)from the\noriginal one.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-1)By virtue of these advantages, the SI scheme has been widely applied to the\ncalculations of long-term evolution of chaotic Hamiltonian systems ranging from the[Kepler problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kepler_problem)to the\nclassical and semi-classical simulations in[molecular dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_dynamics).\n\nMost of the usual numerical methods, such as the primitive[Euler scheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_integration)and the classical[Runge–Kutta\nscheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runge%E2%80%93Kutta_methods), are not symplectic integrators.\n\n## Methods for constructing symplectic algorithms[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=2)]\n\n### Splitting methods for separable Hamiltonians[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=3)]\n\nA widely used class of symplectic integrators is formed by the splitting methods.\n\nAssume that the Hamiltonian is separable, meaning that it can be written in the formH(p,q)=T(p)+V(q).{\\displaystyle H(p,q)=T(p)+V(q).}1\n\nThis happens frequently in Hamiltonian mechanics, withTbeing the[kinetic energy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinetic_energy)andVthe[potential\nenergy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potential_energy).\n\nFor the notational simplicity, let us introduce the symbolz=(q,p){\\displaystyle z=(q,p)}to denote the canonical coordinates including both the position and\nmomentum coordinates. Then, the set of the Hamilton's equations given in the introduction can be expressed in a single expression asz˙={z,H(z)},{\\displaystyle\n{\\dot {z}}=\\{z,H(z)\\},}2\n\nwhere{⋅,⋅}{\\displaystyle \\{\\cdot ,\\cdot \\}}is a[Poisson bracket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson_bracket). Furthermore, by introducing an\noperatorDH⋅={⋅,H}{\\displaystyle D_{H}\\cdot =\\{\\cdot ,H\\}}, which returns a[Poisson bracket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson_bracket)of the operand with\nthe[Hamiltonian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_mechanics#Mathematical_formalism), the expression of the Hamilton's equation can be further\nsimplified to\n\nz˙=DHz.{\\displaystyle {\\dot {z}}=D_{H}z.}\n\nThe formal solution of this set of equations at timet{\\displaystyle t}is given as a[matrix\nexponential](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matrix_exponential):z(t)=exp⁡(tDH)z(0).{\\displaystyle z(t)=\\exp(tD_{H})\\,z(0).}3\n\nNote the positivity oftDH{\\displaystyle tD_{H}}in the matrix exponential.\n\nWhen the Hamiltonian has the form of equation (**[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_1)**), the solution\n(**[3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_3)**) is equivalent toz(t)=exp⁡[t(DT+DV)]z(0).{\\displaystyle z(t)=\\exp\n\\left[t(D_{T}+D_{V})\\right]\\,z(0).}4\n\nThe SI scheme approximates the time-evolution operatorexp⁡[t(DT+DV)]{\\displaystyle \\exp[t(D_{T}+D_{V})]}in the formal solution\n(**[4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_4)**) by a product of operators\nasexp⁡[t(DT+DV)]=∏i=k,…,1exp⁡(tdiDT)exp⁡(tciDV)+O(tk+1)=exp⁡(tdkDT)exp⁡(tckDV)⋯exp⁡(td1DT)exp⁡(tc1DV)+O(tk+1),{\\displaystyle\n{\\begin{aligned}\\exp[t(D_{T}+D_{V})]&=\\prod _{i=k,\\dots ,1}\\exp(td_{i}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{i}D_{V})+O(t^{k+1})\\\\&=\\exp(td_{k}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{k}D_{V})\\cdots\n\\exp(td_{1}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{1}D_{V})+O(t^{k+1}),\\end{aligned}}}5\n\nwhereci{\\displaystyle c_{i}}anddi{\\displaystyle d_{i}}are real numbers,k{\\displaystyle k}is an integer, which is called the order of the integrator, and"}]}, "tool_receipts": [{"id": "tool-receipt:8bc2b43ed488b2bebc4526e8", "attempt_id": "attempt:22da415b81a4f067ba9b9285", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "search", "arguments": {"query": "symplectic integration numerical conservation semi-major axis long-term celestial mechanics", "max_results": 5}, "result": "## Search Results (5 results, 2038ms)\n\n### 1. Symplectic integrators for long-term integrations in celestial ...\n- **URL**: https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF00048485.pdf\n- We discuss the use of symplectic integration algorithms in long-term integrations in the field of celestial mechanics. The methods' advantages and disadvantages (with respect to more common integration methods) are discussed. The numerical performance of the algorithms is evaluated using the 2-body and circular restricted 3-body problems. Symplectic integration methods have the advantages of linear phase error growth in the 2-body problem (unlike most other methods), good conservation of the integrals of the motion, good performance for moderately eccentric orbits, and ease of use. Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy - We discuss the use of symplectic integration algorithms in long-term integrations in the field of celestial mechanics. The methods' advantages and... Yoshida, H.: 1990, ‘Conserved quantities of symplectic integrators for Hamiltonian systems’, preprint, submitted to Physica D. Zhong, G. and Marsden, J. E.: 1988, Phys. Lett. A. 133, 134. ... Astronomy Group, Dept. of Physics, Queen's University, K7L 3N6, Kingston, ON, Canada ... Dept. of Physics, University of Alberta, T6G 2J1, Edmonton, AB, Canada ... Gladman, B., Duncan, M. & Candy, J. Symplectic integrators for long-term integrations in celestial mechanics. Fox, K.: 1984, Celestial Mechanics 33, 127. ... Gladman, B. and Duncan, M.: 1990, Astron. J. 100, 1680. ... Goldstein, H.: 1980, Classical Mechanics, Addison-Wesley, Don Mills, Ontario. ... Henon, M.: 1983, in Chaotic Behaviour of Deterministic Systems, Iooss, G., et al. (eds.), North-Holland Publishing Company, p. 54. Kinoshita, H., Yoshida, H., and Nakai, H.: 1990, ‘Symplectic integrators and application to dynamical astronomy’, preprint.\n\n### 2. (PDF) Symplectic integrators for long-term integrations in celestial ...\n- **URL**: https://www.academia.edu/22201129/Symplectic_integrators_for_long_term_integrations_in_celestial_mechanics\n- We discuss the use of symplectic integration algorithms in long-term integrations in the field of celestial mechanics. The methods' advantages and disadvantages (with respect to more common integration methods) are discussed. The numerical performance of the algorithms is evaluated using the 2-body and circular restricted 3-body problems. Symplectic integration methods have the advantages of linear phase error growth in the 2-body problem (unlike most other methods), good conservation of the integrals of the motion, good performance for moderately eccentric orbits, and ease of use. Symplectic integrators maintain linear phase error growth in long-term celestial mechanics simulations. SIA4 requires three force evaluations per time step, while SIA6 requires nine. These integrators conservatively preserve phase space structures, unlike conventional ODE integrators. Traditional ODE integrators can lead to drift across energy manifolds, violating conservation laws. What are the implications of integrating highly eccentric orbits with symplectic integrators?add · The study indicates that symplectic integrators struggle with highly eccentric orbits due to energy error growth unless a very small step size is used. This can lead to significant roundoff errors and limitations in accuracy. How do symplectic integrators handle numerical roundoff errors?add Symplectic integrators conserve angular momentum exactly, exhibiting remarkably small errors in long-term integrations of the two-body problem.View claim detailsarrow_forward · The SIA6 algorithm shows expected errors of 3 x 10^-14 over long integrations, limited by roundoff effects due to numerical coefficients.View claim detailsarrow_forward\n\n### 3. Symplectic integrators for long-term integrations in celestial ...\n- **URL**: https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1991CeMDA..52..221G/abstract\n- We discuss the use of symplectic integration algorithms in long-term integrations in the field of celestial mechanics. The methods' advantages and disadvantages (with respect to more common integration methods) are discussed. The numerical performance of the algorithms is evaluated using the 2-body and circular restricted 3-body problems. Symplectic integration methods have the advantages of linear phase error growth in the 2-body problem (unlike most other methods), good conservation of the integrals of the motion, good performance for moderately eccentric orbits, and ease of use.\n\n### 4. Symplectic integrator - Wikipedia\n- **URL**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator\n- Symplectic integrators possess, as a conserved quantity, a Hamiltonian which is slightly perturbed from the original one. By virtue of these advantages, the SI scheme has been widely applied to the calculations of long-term evolution of chaotic Hamiltonian systems ranging from the Kepler problem to the classical and semi-classical simulations in molecular dynamics. Most of the usual numerical methods, such as the primitive Euler scheme and the classical Runge–Kutta scheme, are not symplectic integrators. Later on, Blanes and Moan further developed partitioned Runge–Kutta methods for the integration of systems with separable Hamiltonians with very small error constants. Numerov's method, developed by the Russian astronomer Boris Vasil'evich Numerov in 1924, is also a fourth-order symplectic integrator. Hairer, Ernst; Lubich, Christian; Wanner, Gerhard (2006). Geometric Numerical Integration: Structure-Preserving Algorithms for Ordinary Differential Equations (2 ed.). Springer. ISBN ... Kang, Feng; Qin, Mengzhao (2010). Symplectic geometric algorithms for Hamiltonian systems. Symplectic integrators are designed for the numerical solution of Hamilton's equations, which read ... {\\displaystyle {\\dot {p}}=-{\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial q}}\\quad {\\mbox{and}}\\quad {\\dot {q}}={\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial p}},} where ... {\\displaystyle (q,p)} are called canonical coordinates. (See Hamiltonian mechanics for more background.) The time evolution of Hamilton's equations is a symplectomorphism, meaning that it conserves the symplectic 2-form\n\n### 5. Symplectic integrator — Grokipedia\n- **URL**: https://grokipedia.com/page/Symplectic_integrator\n- A symplectic integrator is a numerical integration scheme designed for solving the ordinary differential equations arising from Hamiltonian systems, which preserves the symplectic structure of phase space to ensure accurate long-term behavior in simulations of conservative dynamical systems.[1] This preservation means the method maintains the volume of phase space and the symplectic form, leading to bounded energy errors over extended time intervals, unlike general-purpose integrators that may exhibit artificial energy drift.[2] Originating from foundational work in geometric mechanics, such a While these methods offer superior accuracy for demanding problems like high-precision celestial mechanics, their computational cost scales with the number of stages—often 7-15 force evaluations per step—making them less efficient than lower-order alternatives unless very small time steps are needed for accuracy. Optimized variants reduce stages to 11 in some cases, balancing cost and precision.[25] Numerical demonstrations underscore the stability advantages of these higher-order symplectic integrators. Unlike non-symplectic schemes, which can lead to unphysical drift in energy and phase errors that accumulate, symplectic integrators exhibit bounded energy fluctuations and nearly linear phase errors, making them essential for applications requiring fidelity to the underlying Hamiltonian structure. This preservation of qualitative behavior is particularly crucial in fields like celestial mechanics and accelerator physics.[5][6] The modern development of symplectic integrators gained momentum in the 1980s, driven by challenges in accelerator physics, with seminal contributions including Ronald D. GG is the gravitational constant.[28] This formulation captures the kinetic and potential energies of interacting point masses under Newtonian gravity, making it central to simulations of planetary systems and stellar dynamics.[28] A seminal advancement in simulating such systems is the Wisdom-Holman mapping, which employs recursive splitting of the Hamiltonian to handle planetary perturbations around a dominant central body, such as the Sun.[29] By transforming to heliocentric coordinates, the Hamiltonian becomes separable into Keplerian motion around the central body and perturbative interac\n", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:16:28.546249+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:d716235550d13722523690d9", "attempt_id": "attempt:22da415b81a4f067ba9b9285", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "search", "arguments": {"query": "numerical versus physical orbital drift conservative Newtonian two-body floating-point tolerance", "max_results": 5}, "result": "## Search Results (5 results, 316ms)\n\n### 1. NUMERICAL COMPARISON OF TWO-BODY REGULARIZATIONS\n- **URL**: https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1086/518165/pdf\n- NUMERICAL COMPARISON OF TWO-BODY REGULARIZATIONS Toshio Fukushima National Astronomical Observatory, Ohsawa, Mitaka, Tokyo 181-8588, Japan; toshio.fukushima@nao.ac.jp Received 2007 February 18; accepted 2007 March 15 ABSTRACT We numerically compare four schemes to regularize a three-dimensional two-body problem under perturba tions: the Sperling-Bu¨rdet (S-B), Kustaanheimo-Stiefel (K-S), and Bu¨rdet-Ferrandiz (B-F ) regularizations, and a three-dimensional extension of the Levi-Civita (L-C ) regularization we developed recently. As for the integration time of the equation of motion, the least time is needed for the unregularized treatment, followed by the K-S, the extended L-C, the B-F, and the S-B regularizations. However, these differences become significantly smaller when the time to evaluate perturbations becomes dominant. As for the integration error after one close encounter, the K-S and the extended L-C regularizations are tied for the least error, followed by the S-B, the B-F, and finally the unregularized scheme for unperturbed orbits with eccentricity less than 2. This order is not changed significantly by various kinds of perturbations. As for the integration error of elliptical orbits after multiple orbital periods, the situation remains the same except for the rank of the S-B scheme, which varies from the best to the second worst depending on the length of integration and/or on the nature of perturbations. Also, we confirm that Kepler energy scaling enhances the perfor mance of the unregularized, K-S, and extended L-C schemes. As a result, the K-S and the extended L-C regulariza tions with Kepler energy scaling provide the best cost performance in integrating almost all the perturbed two-body problems. Key words: celestial mechanics — methods: numerical 1. INTRODUCTION Two-body regularization is an efficient tool to integrate perturbed two-body problems numerically. This is true not only in astro dynamics (Bond & Allman 1996) but also in N-body simulations (Aarseth 2003). Originally it was developed to avoid the numer ical difficulty in integrating nearly parabolic orbits such as those of comets. However, its effectiveness was confirmed even for nearly circular orbits (Arakida & Fukushima 2000). This is due to its better numerical stability than unregularized Keplerian mo tion (Stiefel & Scheifele 1971). There are four schemes of three-dimensional two-body regu larization. They are, in chronological order of publication, (1) the Sperling-Bu¨rdet (S-B) regularization (Sperling 1961; Bu¨rdet 1967, 1968), which is also known as the Bu¨rdet-Heggie regulari zation in the field of N-body simulation (Heggie 1973), (2) the Kustaanheimo-Stiefel (K-S) regularization (Kustaanheimo & Stiefel 1965), (3) the Bu¨rdet-Ferrandiz (B-F) regularization (Bu¨rdet 1969; Ferrandiz 1988), and (4) an extension of the Levi-Civita (L-C ) regularization that we developed recently (Fukushima 2007). See Table 1 of Fukushima (2007) for a summary. Recently a simple technique called Kepler energy scaling ( Fukushima 2003) has been recognized as enhancing the per formance of orbit integrations with minimal increase in com putational labor. So we consider three modifications, too: (5) the unregularized treatment with Kepler energy scaling (Fukushima 2003), (6) the K-S regularization with Kepler energy scaling (Fukushima 2004), and (7) the extended L-C regularization with Kepler energy scaling, described in Appendix B4 of Fukushima (2007). In this report we present numerical comparisons of these schemes together with the unregularized formulation in the light of their computational cost and performance. All the numerical experi ments given here are conducted on a PC with an Intel Pentium M chip of 1.3 GHz clock speed; the programming language used is the Compaq FORTRAN 6.6B compiler under Microsoft Windows XP Pro SP2, and the computing environment is double precision with a machine epsilon of 2 53 1:11 ; 10 16. 2. COMPARISON 2.1. Computational T\n\n### 2. Numerical integration methods for orbital motion\n- **URL**: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00049361\n- Numerical integration methods for orbital motion | Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy [Skip to main content](#main) [![Springer Nature Link](https://link.springer.com/oscar-static/images/darwin/header/img/logo-springer-nature-link-3149409f62.svg)](https://link.springer.com) [Log in](https://idp.springer.com/auth/personal/springernature?redirect_uri=https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00049361?) # Numerical integration methods for orbital motion * Published:March 1992 * Volume 53, pages 59–69, (1992) * [Cite this article](#citeas) [![](https://media.springernature.com/w72/springer-static/cover-hires/journal/10569?as=webp)Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy](https://link.springer.com/journal/10569)[Aims and scope](https://link.springer.com/journal/10569/aims-and-scope)[Submit manuscript](https://submission.nature.com/new-submission/10569/3) * [O. Montenbruck](#auth-O_-Montenbruck-Aff1)[1](#Aff1) * 1122Accesses * 43Citations * 6Altmetric * [Explore all metrics](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00049361/metrics) ## Abstract The present report compares Runge-Kutta, multistep and extrapolation methods for the numerical integration of ordinary differential equations and assesses their usefulness for orbit computations of solar system bodies or artificial satellites. The scope of earlier studies is extended by including various methods that have been developed only recently. Several performance tests reveal that modern single- and multistep methods can be similarly efficient over a wide range of eccentricities. Multistep methods are still preferable, however, for ephemeris predictions with a large number of dense output points. This is a preview of subscription content,[log in via an institution](https://wayf.springernature.com/?redirect_uri=https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00049361?error=cookies_not_supported&code=8fc33aa2-f1d5-4199-bd57-99fef7177d4b)to check access. ## Access this article [Log in via an institution](https://wayf.springernature.com/?redirect_uri&#x3D;https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00049361?error=cookies_not_supported&code=8fc33aa2-f1d5-4199-bd57-99fef7177d4b) ## Subscribe and save Springer+ from €37.37 /Month * Starting from 10 chapters or articles per month * Access and download chapters and articles from more than 300k books and 2,500 journals * Cancel anytime[View plans](https://link.springer.com/product/springer-plus) ## Buy Now Buy article PDF 39,95 €Price includes VAT (Belgium) Instant access to the full article PDF. [Institutional subscriptions](https://www.springernature.com/gp/librarians/licensing/agc/journals) ### Similar content being viewed by others ![](https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs40328-020-00293-6/MediaObjects/40328_2020_293_Fig1_HTML.png) ### [Numerical solution of ordinary differential equations in geodetic science using adaptive Gauss numerical integration method](https://link.springer.com/10.1007/s40328-020-00293-6?fromPaywallRec=true) Article13 March 2020 ![](https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs42401-024-00328-8/MediaObjects/42401_2024_328_Fig1_HTML.png) ### [AI-driven modeling and control of low earth orbit satellites](https://link.springer.com/10.1007/s42401-024-00328-8?fromPaywallRec=true) Article21 December 2024 ![](https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3Aplaceholder%2Fimages/placeholder-figure-springernature.png) ### [Fast and accurate analyses of spacecraft dynamics using implicit time integration techniques](https://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12555-014-0486-5?fromPaywallRec=true) Article15 April 2016 ### Explore related subjects Discover the latest articles and news from researchers in related subjects, suggested using machine learning. * [Applied Dynamical Systems](https://link.springer.com/subjects/applied-dynamical-systems) * [Computational Mathematics and Numerical Analysis](https://link.springer.com/su\n\n### 3. Stepsize errors in the N-body problem: discerning Mercury’s true possible long-term orbits\n- **URL**: https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08835\n- # Stepsize errors in the N-body problem: discerning Mercury’s true possible long-term orbits Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society. Published: 2021-12-15. 15 citations. ## Authors - David M. Hernandez (Center for Astrophysics Harvard & Smithsonian): h-index 15; 1,341 citations; corresponding author - Richard E. Zeebe (University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa): h-index 58; 18,027 citations - Sam Hadden (University of Toronto): h-index 16; 877 citations ## Topics - Astro and Planetary Science - Stellar, planetary, and galactic studies - Planetary Science and Exploration --- # Stepsize errors in the N-body problem: discerning Mercury’s true possible long-term orbits MNRAS 000, 000–000 (0000) Preprint 15 December 2021 Compiled using MNRAS LATEX style file v3.0 David M. Hernandez 1?, Richard E. Zeebe 2, Sam Hadden 1,3 1 Harvard–Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics, 60 Garden St., MS 51, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA 2 School of Ocean and Earth Science and Technology, University of Hawaii at Manoa, 1000 Pope Road, MSB 629, Honolulu, HI 96822, USA 3 Canadian Institute for Theoretical Astrophysics, University of Toronto, 60 St. George Street, Toronto, ON M5S 3H8, Canada 15 December 2021 ## Abstract Numerical integrations of the Solar System have been carried out for decades. Their results have been used, for example, to determine whether the Solar System is chaotic, whether Mer cury’s orbit is stable, or to help discern Earth’s climate history. We argue that all of the past studies we consider in this work are affected by numerical chaos to different degrees, affecting the possible orbits and instability probability of Mercury, sometimes significantly. We show how to eliminate the effects of numerical chaos by resolving Mercury’s pericentre passage. We also show that several higher order symplectic maps do not exhibit significant differences in resolving pericentre passage of Mercury (at fixed time step), making their advantages suspect for calculating long-term orbits. Resolving pericentre passage affects a wide array of orbital numerical studies, like exoplanet studies, studies of the galactic centre, and other N-body problems. Key words: methods: numerical—celestial mechanics—-planets and satellites: dynamical evolution and stability 1 INTRODUCTION n-body problems (Heggie & Hut 2003) describe the motion of point particles interacting through forces that depend on pairwise dis tances. In astronomy, they are used to model planetary systems, galaxies, or star clusters, among other problems. For planetary sys tems described by conservative Hamiltonian systems, the Lie al gebra structure is often used to derive numerical methods known as symplectic methods (Hairer et al. 2006). The most famous sym plectic method is arguably the Wisdom–Holman (Wisdom & Hol man 1991) method. 1 This mathematical structure is easily gener alized to encompass non-conservative systems (e.g., Tamayo et al. 2020). Symplectic methods suffer from the well-known and studied roundoff errors and truncation errors. However, another source of error, arguably less understood, is that the stepsize chosen is lim ited by stepsize resonances with the physical frequencies in the sys tem (Wisdom & Holman 1992). The onset of chaotic behavior can be qualitatively understood in terms of the overlap of resonances Chirikov (1979). The artificial resonances introduced by the sym plectic map can overlap, introducing an additional source of un physical chaos. Wisdom & Holman (1992) showed how stepsize resonance overlap occurs in a 2 planet system. Resonances occur between the numerical time step and the difference of the planets’ mean motions. Rauch & Holman (1999) studied numerical chaos in the context of the Stark Problem. Wisdom (2015) further showed how resolving pericentre passage avoids effects of stepsize resonances for another two planet system and developed a simple criterion for the timestep required for pericentre resolution. Hernandez et al. (2020) studied stati\n\n### 4. NUMERICAL ERROR COMPARISONS FOR INTEGRATION\n- **URL**: https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700018976/downloads/19700018976.pdf\n- NUMERICAL ERROR COMPARISONS FOR INTEGRATION OF NEAR EARTH ORBITS IN VARIOUS COORDINATE SYSTEMS by Oliver Albert Schwausch, B.S. THE UNIVERSITY OF TEXAS ENGINEERING MECHANICS RESEARCH LABORATORY Austin, Texas January, 1968 CC (p(COD)I , (NASA CR Ol TMKOR AD NUMBER) (CAIEGORY) Reproduced by the CLEAR.INGHOUSE for Federal Scientific & Technical Information Springfield Va. 22151 0 9 0000 N70-28286 NUMERICAL ERROR COM'PARIO,..S FOR INTEGRATION OF NEAR EARTH ORBITS IN VARIOUS COORDINATE SYSTEMS Oliver Albert Schwausch The University of Texas Austin, Texas January 1968 ... Distribu.ed. .. 'to fost romote the nainseconomic development and technological advancement.' 0.0 * U.S. DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE 00* This document has been approved for public release and sale. This report was prepared under Grant NsG-551 for the National Aeronautics and Space Administration by the Engineering Mechanics Research Laboratory The University of Texas Austin, Texas Dr. Wallace T. Fowler Assistant Professor of Aerospace Engineering ii PREFACE In order to accurately predict the stite (i.e., position and velocity) of a near-Earth satellite, the equations governing the satellite's motion must be integrated nuferically because the pertur bations produced by atmospheric drag, an oblate Earth and celestial bodies preclude the finding of a closed form solution. Generally, the integrated states differ from the actual states, and these differences (state deviations or errors) increase with time. These state devia tions are influenced to a high degree by the coordinate system in which the equations of motion are expressed. Since the two-body influence dominates the satellite's motion and since a closed form snlutiHn to The two-body nroblem is available, an analysis of how two body motion is'affected by different coordinate systems would provide some indication as to how the more complicated perturbed motion is affected by coordinate system choice. The purpose of this investigation is to determine the influence which various coordinate systems have upon the error in the numerically determined value of the state for two-body motion using several integration step sizes and orbit shapes. This is accom plished by numerically integrating the equations of motion for each coordinate system under investigation. At specified time intervals an error check is made by transforming the respective position and velocity vectors to a reference coordinate system. Then a comparison of the integrated state and a reference state generated using the iii solution of the two-body equation is made. In this manner the relative state errors for each system can be obtained, and the res pective error norms computed. After varying the integration step size and the orbit shape, the error norms are analyzed in order to determine the sensitivity of the relative error magnitudes to step size, orbit shape and coordinate system choice. The author wishes to express his sincere appreciation to Dr. W. T. Fowler for bringing this area of study to, the author's attention, for his help and guidance throughout the course of this study and for serving as the supervising professor. The author wishes to thank Dr. B. D. Tapley and Dr. D. G. Hull for serving on the supervising committee, Dr. G. J. Lastman for his valuable sug gestions and advice, Mr. Jack Hart for initial programming assistance and Mr. Jim Wiley for checking the equations of motion. Finally, the author wishes to thank his parents for their help and encouragement throughout his education and particularly his wife, Janice, for her love, patience and understanding. The author is pleased to acknowledge the support of the National Aeronautics and Space Administration under Grant NSG-SS1. Oliver A Schwausch The University of Texas at Austin Austin, Texas November, 1968 iv TABLE OF CONT NTI'S Page PREFACE . iii LIST OF FIGURES . vii LIST OF TABLES ... viii NOMENCLATURE ... ix CHAPTER I - IN\n\n### 5. The Long-term Error Estimation Method for the Numerical Integrations of Celestial Orbits ☆\n- **URL**: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0275106223000061\n- The Long-term Error Estimation Method for the Numerical Integrations of Celestial Orbits - ScienceDirect [Skip to main content](#screen-reader-main-content)[Skip to article](#screen-reader-main-title) [![Elsevier logo](https://sdfestaticassets-us-east-1.sciencedirectassets.com/shared-assets/24/images/elsevier-non-solus-new-grey.svg)ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/) [My account](https://www.sciencedirect.com/user/login?targetURL=/science/article/pii/S0275106223000061&from=globalheader) [Sign in](https://www.sciencedirect.com/user/institution/login?targetURL=/science/article/pii/S0275106223000061) * [Access through**your organization**](https://www.sciencedirect.com/user/institution/login?targetUrl=/science/article/pii/S0275106223000061) * [Purchase PDF](https://www.sciencedirect.com/getaccess/pii/S0275106223000061/purchase) Search ScienceDirect ## Article preview * [Abstract](#preview-section-abstract) * [Introduction](#preview-section-introduction) * [Section snippets](#preview-section-snippets) * [References (31)](#preview-section-references) [![Elsevier](https://sdfestaticassets-us-east-1.sciencedirectassets.com/prod/44c3817e58b49348a73e63fb998fb7b2924522e1/image/elsevier-non-solus.png)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/chinese-astronomy-and-astrophysics) ## [Chinese Astronomy and Astrophysics](https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/chinese-astronomy-and-astrophysics) [Volume 47, Issue 1](https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/chinese-astronomy-and-astrophysics/vol/47/issue/1),January–March 2023, Pages 177-203 [![Chinese Astronomy and Astrophysics](https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0275106223X00022-cov150h.gif)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/chinese-astronomy-and-astrophysics/vol/47/issue/1) # The Long-term Error Estimation Method for the Numerical Integrations of Celestial Orbits[☆](#aep-article-footnote-id1) Author links open overlay panelSongHao-ran,HuangWei-dong Show more Add to Mendeley Share Cite [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chinastron.2023.03.005](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chinastron.2023.03.005)[Get rights and content](https://s100.copyright.com/AppDispatchServlet?publisherName=ELS&contentID=S0275106223000061&orderBeanReset=true) ## Abstract [Numerical methods](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/earth-and-planetary-sciences/mathematical-method)have become a very important type of tool for[celestial mechanics](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/earth-and-planetary-sciences/celestial-mechanics), especially in the study of planetary[ephemerides](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/physics-and-astronomy/ephemerides). The errors generated during the computation are hard to know beforehand when applying a certain[numerical integrator](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/numerical-integrator)to solve a certain orbit. In that case, it is not easy to design a certain integrator for a certain celestial case when the requirement of accuracy were extremely high or the time-span of the integration were extremely large. Especially when a fixed-step method is applied, the caution and effort it takes would always be tremendous in finding a suitable time-step, because it is about whether the accuracy and time-cost of the final result are acceptable. Thus, finding the best balance between efficiency and accuracy with the least time cost appeared to be a major obstruction in the face of both[numerical integrator](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/numerical-integrator)designers and their users. To solve this problem, we investigate the variation pattern of[truncation error](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/truncation-error)and the pattern of rounding error distributions with time-step and time-span of the integration. According to those patterns, we promote an error estimation method that could predict the distribution of rounding errors and the total truncation errors with any time-step at any time-spot with little experimental cost, and test it with the\n", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:16:34.584562+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:de79dd690c18747506b9d70b", "attempt_id": "attempt:22da415b81a4f067ba9b9285", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF00048485.pdf"}, "result": "## Client Challenge\n\n**Source**: https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF00048485.pdf\n\n---\n\nA required part of this site couldn’t load. This may be due to a browser extension, network issues, or browser settings. Please check your connection, disable any ad blockers, or try using a different browser.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:16:40.421435+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:ef73d790986dd5a45404982f", "attempt_id": "attempt:22da415b81a4f067ba9b9285", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://www.academia.edu/22201129/Symplectic_integrators_for_long_term_integrations_in_celestial_mechanics"}, "result": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:16:48.474152+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:9bf3c797439a37b718f24979", "attempt_id": "attempt:22da415b81a4f067ba9b9285", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1991CeMDA..52..221G/abstract"}, "result": "extract_invalid_content\nResponse content is not valid for extraction.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:16:54.117959+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:07e264f275e6df35297d1790", "attempt_id": "attempt:22da415b81a4f067ba9b9285", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator"}, "result": "## Symplectic integrator - Wikipedia\n\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator\n\n---\n\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#bodyContent)\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nNumerical integration scheme for Hamiltonian systems\n\nIn[mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematics), a**symplectic integrator**(**SI**) is a[numerical integration scheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_ordinary_differential_equations)for[Hamiltonian systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_system). Symplectic integrators form the subclass of[geometric integrators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geometric_integrator)which, by definition, are[canonical transformations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonical_transformation). They are widely used in[nonlinear dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonlinear_dynamics),[molecular dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_dynamics),[discrete element methods](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_element_method),[accelerator physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle_accelerator),[plasma physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plasma_physics),[quantum physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_physics), and[celestial mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celestial_mechanics).\n\n## Introduction[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=1)]\n\nSymplectic integrators are designed for the numerical solution of[Hamilton's equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton's_equations), which readp˙=−∂H∂qandq˙=∂H∂p,{\\displaystyle {\\dot {p}}=-{\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial q}}\\quad {\\mbox{and}}\\quad {\\dot {q}}={\\frac {\\partial H}{\\partial p}},}whereq{\\displaystyle q}denotes the position coordinates,p{\\displaystyle p}the momentum coordinates, andH{\\displaystyle H}is the Hamiltonian. The set of position and momentum coordinates(q,p){\\displaystyle (q,p)}are called[canonical coordinates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonical_coordinates). (See[Hamiltonian mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_mechanics)for more background.)\n\nThe[time evolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_evolution)of[Hamilton's equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton's_equations)is a[symplectomorphism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectomorphism), meaning that it conserves the symplectic[2-form](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2-form)dp∧dq{\\displaystyle dp\\wedge dq}. A numerical scheme is a symplectic integrator if it also conserves this 2-form.\n\nSymplectic integrators possess, as a conserved quantity, a Hamiltonian which is slightly[perturbed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perturbation_theory)from the original one.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-1)By virtue of these advantages, the SI scheme has been widely applied to the calculations of long-term evolution of chaotic Hamiltonian systems ranging from the[Kepler problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kepler_problem)to the classical and semi-classical simulations in[molecular dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_dynamics).\n\nMost of the usual numerical methods, such as the primitive[Euler scheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_integration)and the classical[Runge–Kutta scheme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runge%E2%80%93Kutta_methods), are not symplectic integrators.\n\n## Methods for constructing symplectic algorithms[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=2)]\n\n### Splitting methods for separable Hamiltonians[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=3)]\n\nA widely used class of symplectic integrators is formed by the splitting methods.\n\nAssume that the Hamiltonian is separable, meaning that it can be written in the formH(p,q)=T(p)+V(q).{\\displaystyle H(p,q)=T(p)+V(q).}1\n\nThis happens frequently in Hamiltonian mechanics, withTbeing the[kinetic energy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinetic_energy)andVthe[potential energy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potential_energy).\n\nFor the notational simplicity, let us introduce the symbolz=(q,p){\\displaystyle z=(q,p)}to denote the canonical coordinates including both the position and momentum coordinates. Then, the set of the Hamilton's equations given in the introduction can be expressed in a single expression asz˙={z,H(z)},{\\displaystyle {\\dot {z}}=\\{z,H(z)\\},}2\n\nwhere{⋅,⋅}{\\displaystyle \\{\\cdot ,\\cdot \\}}is a[Poisson bracket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson_bracket). Furthermore, by introducing an operatorDH⋅={⋅,H}{\\displaystyle D_{H}\\cdot =\\{\\cdot ,H\\}}, which returns a[Poisson bracket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson_bracket)of the operand with the[Hamiltonian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_mechanics#Mathematical_formalism), the expression of the Hamilton's equation can be further simplified to\n\nz˙=DHz.{\\displaystyle {\\dot {z}}=D_{H}z.}\n\nThe formal solution of this set of equations at timet{\\displaystyle t}is given as a[matrix exponential](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matrix_exponential):z(t)=exp⁡(tDH)z(0).{\\displaystyle z(t)=\\exp(tD_{H})\\,z(0).}3\n\nNote the positivity oftDH{\\displaystyle tD_{H}}in the matrix exponential.\n\nWhen the Hamiltonian has the form of equation (**[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_1)**), the solution (**[3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_3)**) is equivalent toz(t)=exp⁡[t(DT+DV)]z(0).{\\displaystyle z(t)=\\exp \\left[t(D_{T}+D_{V})\\right]\\,z(0).}4\n\nThe SI scheme approximates the time-evolution operatorexp⁡[t(DT+DV)]{\\displaystyle \\exp[t(D_{T}+D_{V})]}in the formal solution (**[4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_4)**) by a product of operators asexp⁡[t(DT+DV)]=∏i=k,…,1exp⁡(tdiDT)exp⁡(tciDV)+O(tk+1)=exp⁡(tdkDT)exp⁡(tckDV)⋯exp⁡(td1DT)exp⁡(tc1DV)+O(tk+1),{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\exp[t(D_{T}+D_{V})]&=\\prod _{i=k,\\dots ,1}\\exp(td_{i}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{i}D_{V})+O(t^{k+1})\\\\&=\\exp(td_{k}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{k}D_{V})\\cdots \\exp(td_{1}D_{T})\\exp(tc_{1}D_{V})+O(t^{k+1}),\\end{aligned}}}5\n\nwhereci{\\displaystyle c_{i}}anddi{\\displaystyle d_{i}}are real numbers,k{\\displaystyle k}is an integer, which is called the order of the integrator, and where∑i=1kci=∑i=1kdi=1{\\textstyle \\sum _{i=1}^{k}c_{i}=\\sum _{i=1}^{k}d_{i}=1}. Note that each of the operatorsexp⁡(tciDV){\\displaystyle \\exp(tc_{i}D_{V})}andexp⁡(tdiDT){\\displaystyle \\exp(td_{i}D_{T})}provides a[symplectic map](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectomorphism), so their product appearing in the right-hand side of (**[5](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_5)**) also constitutes a symplectic map.\n\nSinceDT2z={{z,T},T}={(q˙,0),T}=(0,0){\\displaystyle D_{T}^{2}z=\\{\\{z,T\\},T\\}=\\{({\\dot {q}},0),T\\}=(0,0)}for allz{\\displaystyle z}, we can conclude thatDT2=0.{\\displaystyle D_{T}^{2}=0.}6\n\nBy using a[Taylor series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taylor_series),exp⁡(aDT){\\displaystyle \\exp(aD_{T})}can be expressed asexp⁡(aDT)=∑n=0∞(aDT)nn!,{\\displaystyle \\exp(aD_{T})=\\sum _{n=0}^{\\infty }{\\frac {{\\left(aD_{T}\\right)}^{n}}{n!}},}7\n\nwherea{\\displaystyle a}is an arbitrary[real number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_number). Combining (**[6](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_6)**) and (**[7](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#math_7)**), and by using the same reasoning forDV{\\displaystyle D_{V}}as we have used forDT{\\displaystyle D_{T}}, we get{exp⁡(aDV)=1+aDV,exp⁡(aDT)=1+aDT.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{cases}\\exp(aD_{V})=1+aD_{V},\\\\[2pt]\\exp(aD_{T})=1+aD_{T}.\\end{cases}}}8\n\nIn concrete terms,exp⁡(tciDV){\\displaystyle \\exp(tc_{i}D_{V})}gives the mapping\n\n(qp)↦(qp−tci∂V∂q(q)),{\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}q\\\\p\\end{pmatrix}}\\mapsto {\\begin{pmatrix}q\\\\p-tc_{i}{\\frac {\\partial V}{\\partial q}}(q)\\end{pmatrix}},}\n\nandexp⁡(tdiDT){\\displaystyle \\exp(td_{i}D_{T})}gives\n\n(qp)↦(q+tdi∂T∂p(p)p).{\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}q\\\\p\\end{pmatrix}}\\mapsto {\\begin{pmatrix}q+td_{i}{\\frac {\\partial T}{\\partial p}}(p)\\\\p\\end{pmatrix}}.}\n\nNote that both of these maps are practically computable.\n\n#### Algorithm[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=4)]\n\nIn each time step, the general algorithm for this class of symplectic integrators maps some initial state(q0,p0){\\displaystyle (q_{0},p_{0})}to the state(qk,pk){\\displaystyle (q_{k},p_{k})}one timestept{\\displaystyle t}later. This is done through the sequence of substeps(q0,p0)↦(q1,p1)↦⋯↦(qk−1,pk−1)↦(qk,pk){\\displaystyle (q_{0},p_{0})\\mapsto (q_{1},p_{1})\\mapsto \\dots \\mapsto (q_{k-1},p_{k-1})\\mapsto (q_{k},p_{k})}. For everyi=1,…,k{\\displaystyle i=1,\\dots ,k}, the following equations are used to compute the substep(qi−1,pi−1)↦(qi,pi){\\displaystyle (q_{i-1},p_{i-1})\\mapsto (q_{i},p_{i})}:\n\npi=pi−1+tciF(qi−1)qi=qi−1+tdipim{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}p_{i}&=p_{i-1}+tc_{i}F(q_{i-1})\\\\[1ex]q_{i}&=q_{i-1}+td_{i}{\\frac {p_{i}}{m}}\\end{aligned}}}\n\nwhereq{\\displaystyle q}is the position,p{\\displaystyle p}is the momentum,F(q)=−∂V∂q(q){\\displaystyle F(q)=-{\\tfrac {\\partial V}{\\partial q}}(q)}is the force vector atq{\\displaystyle q}, andm{\\displaystyle m}is the scalar quantity of mass.\n\nEquivalently, after converting into Lagrangian coordinates, each substep becomes\n\nvi=vi−1+tcia(xi−1)xi=xi−1+tdivi{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}v_{i}&=v_{i-1}+tc_{i}a(x_{i-1})\\\\[1ex]x_{i}&=x_{i-1}+td_{i}v_{i}\\end{aligned}}}\n\nwherex{\\displaystyle x}is the position,v{\\displaystyle v}is the velocity, anda(x){\\displaystyle a(x)}is the acceleration vector atx{\\displaystyle x}.\n\nSeveral symplectic integrators are given below.\n\n#### A first-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=5)]\n\nThe[symplectic Euler method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_Euler_method)is the first-order integrator withk=1{\\displaystyle k=1}and coefficientsc1=d1=1.{\\displaystyle c_{1}=d_{1}=1.}\n\nNote that the algorithm above does not work if time-reversibility is needed. The algorithm has to be implemented in two parts, one for positive time steps, one for negative time steps.\n\n#### A second-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=6)]\n\nThe[Verlet method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verlet_integration)is the second-order symplectic integrator withk=2{\\displaystyle k=2}and coefficients\n\nc1=12,d1=1,c2=12,d2=0.{\\displaystyle c_{1}={\\tfrac {1}{2}},\\qquad d_{1}=1,\\qquad c_{2}={\\tfrac {1}{2}},\\qquad d_{2}=0.}\n\nNote that the coefficients\n\nc1=0,d1=12,c2=1,d2=12{\\displaystyle c_{1}=0,\\qquad d_{1}={\\tfrac {1}{2}},\\qquad c_{2}=1,\\qquad d_{2}={\\tfrac {1}{2}}}\n\nalso form a second-order symplectic integrator[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-forest1990-2)[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-yoshida1990-3), which is the Verlet method but shifted by half a time stept{\\displaystyle t}.\n\nSinced2=0{\\displaystyle d_{2}=0}in the first set of coefficients andc1=0{\\displaystyle c_{1}=0}in the second set of coefficients, in both algorithms above one update can be ignored. There are thus only 3 steps to the algorithm, and step 1 and 3 are exactly the same, leading to the algorithm being symmetric in time, so the positive time version can also be used for negative time.\n\n#### A third-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=7)]\n\nA third-order symplectic integrator (withk=3{\\displaystyle k=3}) was discovered by Ronald Ruth in 1983.[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-4)One of the many solutions is given byc1=724,c2=34,c3=−124,d1=23,d2=−23,d3=1.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}c_{1}&={\\tfrac {7}{24}},&c_{2}&={\\tfrac {3}{4}},&c_{3}&=-{\\tfrac {1}{24}},\\\\[1ex]d_{1}&={\\tfrac {2}{3}},&d_{2}&=-{\\tfrac {2}{3}},&d_{3}&=1.\\end{aligned}}}\n\n#### A fourth-order example[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=8)]\n\nA fourth-order integrator (withk=4{\\displaystyle k=4}) was also discovered by Ruth in 1983 and distributed privately to the particle-accelerator community at that time. This was described in a lively review article by Forest.[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-5)This fourth-order integrator was published in 1990 by Forest and Ruth and also independently discovered by two other groups around that same time:[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-forest1990-2)[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-yoshida1990-3)[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-6)c1=c4=12(2−21/3),c2=c3=1−21/32(2−21/3),d1=d3=12−21/3,d2=−21/32−21/3,d4=0,{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}c_{1}&=c_{4}={\\frac {1}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}},&c_{2}&=c_{3}={\\frac {1-2^{1/3}}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}},\\\\[1ex]d_{1}&=d_{3}={\\frac {1}{2-2^{1/3}}},&d_{2}&=-{\\frac {2^{1/3}}{2-2^{1/3}}},\\qquad d_{4}=0,\\end{aligned}}}or alternatively,c1=0,c3=−21/32−21/3,c2=c4=12−21/3,d1=d4=12(2−21/3),d2=d3=1−21/32(2−21/3).{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}c_{1}&=0,\\qquad c_{3}=-{\\frac {2^{1/3}}{2-2^{1/3}}},&c_{2}&=c_{4}={\\frac {1}{2-2^{1/3}}},\\\\[1ex]d_{1}&=d_{4}={\\frac {1}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}},&d_{2}&=d_{3}={\\frac {1-2^{1/3}}{2\\left(2-2^{1/3}\\right)}}.\\end{aligned}}}\n\nTo determine these coefficients, the[Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baker%E2%80%93Campbell%E2%80%93Hausdorff_formula)can be used.[Yoshida](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haruo_Yoshida?action=edit&redlink=1), in particular, gives an elegant derivation of coefficients for[higher-order integrators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leapfrog_integration#Yoshida_algorithms).[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-yoshida1990-3)Later on, Blanes and Moan[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-7)further developed partitioned[Runge–Kutta methods](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runge%E2%80%93Kutta_methods)for the integration of systems with separable Hamiltonians with very small error constants.[Numerov's method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerov's_method), developed by the Russian astronomer[Boris Vasil'evich Numerov](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boris_Vasil'evich_Numerov)in 1924, is also a fourth-order symplectic integrator.[[8]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-8)\n\n### Splitting methods for general nonseparable Hamiltonians[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=9)]\n\nGeneral nonseparable Hamiltonians can also be explicitly and symplectically integrated.\n\nTo do so, Tao introduced a restraint that binds two copies of phase space together to enable an explicit splitting of such systems.[[9]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-9)The idea is, instead ofH(Q,P){\\displaystyle H(Q,P)}, one simulatesH¯(q,p,x,y)=H(q,y)+H(x,p)+ω(12‖q−x‖22+12‖p−y‖22),{\\displaystyle {\\bar {H}}(q,p,x,y)=H(q,y)+H(x,p)+\\omega \\left({\\tfrac {1}{2}}{\\left\\|q-x\\right\\|}_{2}^{2}+{\\tfrac {1}{2}}{\\left\\|p-y\\right\\|}_{2}^{2}\\right),}whose solution agrees with that ofH(Q,P){\\displaystyle H(Q,P)}in the sense thatq(t)=x(t)=Q(t){\\displaystyle q(t)=x(t)=Q(t)},p(t)=y(t)=P(t){\\displaystyle p(t)=y(t)=P(t)}.\n\nThe new Hamiltonian is advantageous for explicit symplectic integration, because it can be split into the sum of three sub-Hamiltonians,HA=H(q,y){\\displaystyle H_{A}=H(q,y)},HB=H(x,p){\\displaystyle H_{B}=H(x,p)}, andHC=ω(12‖q−x‖22+12‖p−y‖22){\\textstyle H_{C}=\\omega \\left({\\frac {1}{2}}\\left\\|q-x\\right\\|_{2}^{2}+{\\frac {1}{2}}\\left\\|p-y\\right\\|_{2}^{2}\\right)}. Exact solutions of all three sub-Hamiltonians can be explicitly obtained: bothHA,HB{\\displaystyle H_{A},H_{B}}solutions correspond to shifts of mismatched position and momentum, andHC{\\displaystyle H_{C}}corresponds to a linear transformation. To symplectically simulate the system, one simply composes these solution maps.\n\n## Applications[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=10)]\n\n### In plasma physics[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=11)]\n\nIn recent decades symplectic integrator in plasma physics has become an active research topic,[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-10)because straightforward applications of the standard symplectic methods do not suit the need of large-scale plasma simulations enabled by the peta- to exa-scale computing hardware. Special symplectic algorithms need to be customarily designed, tapping into the special structures of the physics problem under investigation. One such example is the[charged particle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charged_particle)dynamics in an[electromagnetic field](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_field). With the canonical symplectic structure, the Hamiltonian of the dynamics isH(p,x)=12(p−A)2+ϕ,{\\displaystyle H({\\boldsymbol {p}},{\\boldsymbol {x}})={\\tfrac {1}{2}}\\left({\\boldsymbol {p}}-{\\boldsymbol {A}}\\right)^{2}+\\phi ,}whosep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence andx{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {x}}}-dependence are not separable, and standard explicit symplectic methods do not apply. For large-scale simulations on massively parallel clusters, however, explicit methods are preferred. To overcome this difficulty, we can explore the specific way that thep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence andx{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {x}}}-dependence are entangled in this Hamiltonian, and try to design a symplectic algorithm just for this or this type of problem. First, we note that thep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence is quadratic, therefore the first order symplectic Euler method implicit inp{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}is actually explicit. This is what is used in the canonical symplectic[particle-in-cell](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle-in-cell)(PIC) algorithm.[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-11)To build high order explicit methods, we further note that thep{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {p}}}-dependence andx{\\textstyle {\\boldsymbol {x}}}-dependence in thisH(p,x){\\textstyle H({\\boldsymbol {p}},{\\boldsymbol {x}})}are product-separable, 2nd and 3rd order explicit symplectic algorithms can be constructed using generating functions,[[12]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-12)and arbitrarily high-order explicit symplectic integrators for time-dependent electromagnetic fields can also be constructed using Runge-Kutta techniques.[[13]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-13)\n\nA more elegant and versatile alternative is to look at the following non-canonical symplectic structure of the problem,i(x˙,v˙)Ω=−dH,Ω=d(v+A)∧dx,H=12v2+ϕ.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}i_{({\\dot {\\boldsymbol {x}}},{\\dot {\\boldsymbol {v}}})}\\Omega &=-dH,\\\\\\Omega &=d({\\boldsymbol {v}}+{\\boldsymbol {A}})\\wedge d{\\boldsymbol {x}},\\\\[1ex]H&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}{\\boldsymbol {v}}^{2}+\\phi .\\end{aligned}}}HereΩ{\\textstyle \\Omega }is a non-constant non-canonical symplectic form. General symplectic integrator for non-constant non-canonical symplectic structure, explicit or implicit, is not known to exist. However, for this specific problem, a family of high-order explicit non-canonical symplectic integrators can be constructed using the He splitting method.[[14]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-14)SplittingH{\\textstyle H}into 4 parts,H=Hx+Hy+Hz+Hϕ,{\\displaystyle H=H_{x}+H_{y}+H_{z}+H_{\\phi },}Hx=12vx2,Hy=12vy2,Hz=12vz2,Hϕ=ϕ,{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}H_{x}&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}v_{x}^{2},&H_{y}&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}v_{y}^{2},\\\\[2pt]H_{z}&={\\tfrac {1}{2}}v_{z}^{2},&H_{\\phi }&=\\phi ,\\end{aligned}}}we find serendipitously that for each subsystem, e.g.,i(x˙,v˙)Ω=−dHx{\\displaystyle i_{({\\dot {\\boldsymbol {x}}},{\\dot {\\boldsymbol {v}}})}\\Omega =-dH_{x}}andi(x˙,v˙)Ω=−dHϕ,{\\displaystyle i_{({\\dot {\\boldsymbol {x}}},{\\dot {\\boldsymbol {v}}})}\\Omega =-dH_{\\phi },}the solution map can be written down explicitly and calculated exactly. Then explicit high-order non-canonical symplectic algorithms can be constructed using different compositions. LetΘx,Θy,Θz{\\textstyle \\Theta _{x},\\Theta _{y},\\Theta _{z}}andΘϕ{\\textstyle \\Theta _{\\phi }}denote the exact solution maps for the 4 subsystems. A 1st-order symplectic scheme isΘ1(Δτ)=Θx(Δτ)Θy(Δτ)Θz(Δτ)Θϕ(Δτ).{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\Theta _{1}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}=\\Theta _{x}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,\\Theta _{y}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,\\Theta _{z}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,\\Theta _{\\phi }{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\,.\\end{aligned}}}A symmetric 2nd-order symplectic scheme is,Θ2(Δτ)=Θx(Δτ2)Θy(Δτ2)Θz(Δτ2)Θϕ(Δτ)Θz(Δt2)Θy(Δt2)Θx(Δt2),{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\Theta _{2}{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}={}&\\Theta _{x}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta \\tau }{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{y}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta \\tau }{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{z}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta \\tau }{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{\\phi }{\\left(\\Delta \\tau \\right)}\\\\&\\Theta _{z}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta t}{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{y}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta t}{2}}\\right)}\\,\\Theta _{x}{\\left({\\tfrac {\\Delta t}{2}}\\right)},\\end{aligned}}}which is a customarily modified[Strang splitting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strang_splitting). A2(ℓ+1){\\textstyle 2(\\ell +1)}-th order scheme can be constructed from a2ℓ{\\textstyle 2\\ell }-th order scheme using the method of triple jump,Θ2(ℓ+1)(Δτ)=Θ2ℓ(αℓΔτ)Θ2ℓ(βℓΔτ)Θ2ℓ(αℓΔτ),{\\displaystyle \\Theta _{2(\\ell +1)}(\\Delta \\tau )=\\Theta _{2\\ell }(\\alpha _{\\ell }\\Delta \\tau )\\,\\Theta _{2\\ell }(\\beta _{\\ell }\\Delta \\tau )\\,\\Theta _{2\\ell }(\\alpha _{\\ell }\\Delta \\tau )\\,,}αℓ=12−21/(2ℓ+1),βℓ=1−2αℓ.{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\alpha _{\\ell }&={\\frac {1}{2-2^{1/(2\\ell +1)}}},&\\beta _{\\ell }&=1-2\\alpha _{\\ell }\\,.\\end{aligned}}}The He splitting method is one of key techniques used in the structure-preserving geometric[particle-in-cell](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle-in-cell)(PIC) algorithms.[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-15)[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-16)[[17]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-17)[[18]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_note-18)\n\n## See also[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=12)]\n- [Energy drift](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_drift)\n- [Multisymplectic integrator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multisymplectic_integrator)\n- [Variational integrator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variational_integrator)\n- [Verlet integration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verlet_integration)\n\n## References[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Symplectic_integrator&action=edit&section=13)]\n\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_ref-1)Tuckerman, Mark E. (2010).*Statistical Mechanics: Theory and Molecular Simulation*(1ed.). Oxford University Press. pp.121–124.[ISBN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier))[978-0-19-852526-4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-19-852526-4).\n- [1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_ref-forest1990_2-0)[2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_ref-forest1990_2-1)Forest, E.; Ruth, Ronald D. (1990).[\"Fourth-order symplectic integration\"](https://cloudfront.escholarship.org/dist/prd/content/qt35h9v2k9/qt35h9v2k9.pdf)(PDF).*Physica D*.**43**:105–117.[Bibcode](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibcode_(identifier)):[1990PhyD...43..105F](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1990PhyD...43..105F).[doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)):[10.1016/0167-2789(90)90019-L](https://doi.org/10.1016%2F0167-2789%2890%2990019-L).[OSTI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OSTI_(identifier))[1449044](https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1449044).\n- [1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_ref-yoshida1990_3-0)[2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_ref-yoshida1990_3-1)[3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symplectic_integrator#cite_ref-yoshida1990_3-2)Yoshida, H. (1990). \"Construction of higher order symplectic integrators\".*Phys. Lett. A*.**150**(5–7):262–268.[Bibcode](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibcode_(identifier)):[1990PhLA..150..262Y](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1990PhLA..150..262Y).[doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)):[10.1016/0375-960